# Cases skipped with --profile max-sessions-1 in CI; every other case runs.
# The profile changes only the destination's sshd and the runner's
# strict-multiplexing SSH wrapper, which applies only to connections to
# destination. A case that never contacts destination, directly, through a
# script it runs, or through a coordinator on another host, behaves exactly
# as in the default profile. List only such cases, and only when no case that
# still runs needs their setup. New cases run in both profiles unless listed.
privileged copies keep root security checks
ephemeral connect only reuses forward SSH and leaves receiving off
return copies await local approval and denial leaves no destination
approval allows the pending 128-worker copy once
disconnected requests cannot be approved later
changing policy cancels a pending request
native Linux notification actions control return copies
return copies hand off between server PATH builds
remote commands run through the return connection
storage authorization through the return connection
explicit automatic approval supports unattended copies
named return rejects traversal and receiver symlink escape
a copied receiver identity cannot displace a responsive laptop
interrupted named copy fails, laptop reconnects, and retry resumes
return heartbeat timeout reconnects without toggling persistence
cwd permits destinations outside its starting directory
persist receive off/on keeps ordinary persistence and restarts receiving
persistence off stops background receiving
offline names require the original receiver identity or explicit replacement
shell completion adapters parse and keep descriptions separate
remote filename completion reuses a persistent ordinary SSH login
direct SSH helper sees the current SendEnv value
restarting persistence adopts the new environment
remote completion details use remote metadata without persistence
small native push to an ordinary SSH destination takes one turn
persistent debug output closes with the copying process
existing small files skip TCP setup and SSH data sessions
native remote removal follows parents but preserves final link identity
native remote removal requires explicit tree selection
